<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
{#- The public head, as every page in the sitemap has it: a shared link to this page was a
    bare line of text, with no description and no picture (targum-internal#518). One
    address, in English for everybody (David, 2026-11-12), so no `alternates`. -#}
{% include '_public_head.html.j2' %}
{{ styles('tokens.css', 'public-page.css', 'about.css', 'front-bar.css') }}
{{ hebrew_face() }}
{{ chrome_face() }}
</head>
<body class="about">
{#- The front door's bar, as the Library and the text pages have it (design's #477 finish
    bar, P1-2, 2026-11-12): this page stood outside the door, with a lone Sign in and no
    way to the waitlist or the other language. The page has no Russian of its own (David,
    2026-11-12), so the bar's RU is the front door's: `here` is `/`. -#}
{% with bar_class = 'front-bar', home = '/', here = '/', join = '/#join' %}{% include '_front_bar.html.j2' %}{% endwith %}

{#- This page described targum at length: what it does, what had shipped, what it could
    not do yet. None of it was what somebody arriving early needs to be told. The state
    of the thing is the calendar, what was built is a line or three a day under it, and
    the rest is on GitHub. -#}
<main class="sheet">
  <div class="card">
  <h1>{{ t("about.page.targum-is-built-in-public", "targum is built in public") }}</h1>

  {% if work.commits %}
  {#- The two days the count runs between, not "the last 30 days": served from a wheel
      these numbers are as old as the deploy, and the sentence has to stay true for as
      long as the wheel does. It said "in the 30 days to 29 September", which was true
      and was not English (David, 2026-09-29). -#}
  <p class="figures">
    <b>{{ commits }}</b> {{ tn("about.page.changes-between", work.commits, "change from {since} to {through}", "changes from {since} to {through}", since=since, through=through) }}
  </p>
  {#- One square a day. Colour says how much landed, and an empty day is left as the
      quiet grey it was rather than dressed up. -#}
  <ol class="calendar" aria-label="{{ t("about.page.changes-per-day", "Changes per day, last {days} days", days=days) }}">
    {% for day, count in work.days %}
    <li class="day level-{{ level(count, work.busiest) }}"
        title="{{ day }} — {{ count }} {{ 'change' if count == 1 else 'changes' }}"></li>
    {% endfor %}
  </ol>
  <p class="key"><span class="less">{{ t("about.page.less", "Less") }}</span>
    {% for n in [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] %}<i class="day level-{{ n }}"></i>{% endfor %}
    <span class="more">{{ t("about.page.more", "More") }}</span></p>
  {% endif %}

  {% if log %}
  {#- What was built, newest first. The lines are English whatever the page is, so the
      list says so and a screen reader changes voice for it. -#}
  <div class="built" lang="en" dir="ltr">
    {% for day in log %}
    <section class="built-day">
      <h2><time datetime="{{ day.iso }}">{{ day.said }}</time></h2>
      <ul>
        {% for line in day.lines %}
        <li>{{ line }}</li>
        {% endfor %}
      </ul>
    </section>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  {#- The page that shows the hard parts solved (targum-internal#401). -#}
  <p class="log-more"><a href="/how">{{ t("about.page.how-it-reads-hebrew", "How targum reads Hebrew") }}</a></p>
  <p class="log-more">{{ t("about.page.the-work-is-on", "The work is on") }}
    <a href="https://github.com/DLangellotti/targum">GitHub</a>.</p>
  </div>
</main>

{% include '_public_foot.html.j2' %}
</body>
</html>
